{{smaller block|The first of these studies [biology] gives rise to the sciences of anatomy and physiology, as well as to the subsidiary science of pathology. On the other hand, ''Biogeny'' comprises embryology, morphology, and questions relating to the origin of species. ''J. Fiske'', Cosmic Philos., I. 221.}}

'''biographer''' (bī-og′ra̤-fėr), ''n.'' [< ML. ''biographus'' (see ''biography'') + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''philosopher.''] One who writes a biography, or an account of the life and actions of a particular person; a writer of lives.

'''biographic''' (bī-ọ̄-graf′ik), ''a.'' [< ''biography'' + ''-ic.''] Pertaining to or of the nature of biography.
{{smaller block|To all which questions, not unessential in a ''biographic'' work, mere conjecture must for most part return answer.
{{right|''Carlyle'', Sartor Resartus, p. 95.}}}}

'''biographical''' (bī-ọ̄-graf′i-ka̤l), ''a.'' Relating or pertaining to the life of an individual; dealing with or containing biographies: as, ''biographical'' details; a ''biographical'' dictionary.
{{smaller block|The historian should rarely digress into ''biographical'' particulars except in as far as they contribute to the clearness of his narrative of political occurrences.
{{right|''Sir J. Mackintosh'', Sir Thos. More.}}}}

'''biographically''' (bī-ọ̄-graf′i-ka̤l-i), ''adv.'' In a biographical sense or manner; with reference to biography.

'''biographist''' (bī-og′ra̤-fist), ''n.'' [< ''biography'' + ''-ist.''] A biographer. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Want of honest heart in the ''Biographists'' of these Saints . . . betrayed their pens to such abominable untruths.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, iii.}}}}

'''biographize''' (bī-og′ra̤-fīz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''biographized'', ppr. ''biographizing''. [< ''biography'' + ''-ize.''] To write the biography or a history of the life of. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Now do I bless the man who undertook</poem>}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''biography''' (bī-og′ra-fi), ''n.''; pl. ''biographies'' (-fiz). [= F. ''biographie'', < LGr. βιογραφία, biography, < *βιογράφος (> ML. ''biographus'', > F. ''biographe'', a biographer), < Gr. βίος, life, + γράφειν, write.] '''1.''' The history of the life of a particular person.
{{smaller block|There is no heroic poem in the world but is at bottom a ''biography'', the life of a man.
{{right|''Carlyle'', Essays.}}}}
'''2.''' Biographical writing in general, or as a department of literature.
{{smaller block|This, then, was the first great merit of Montesquieu, that he effected a complete separation between ''biography'' and history, and taught historians to study, not the peculiarities of individual character, but the general aspect of the society in which the peculiarities appeared.
{{right|''Buckle'', Civilization, I. xiii.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''nat. hist.'', the life-history of an animal or a plant. ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Biography'', ''Memoir.'' When there is a difference between these words, it may be that ''memoir'' indicates a less complete or minute account of a person’s life, or it may be that the person himself records his own recollections of the past, especially as connected with his own life; in the latter case ''memoir'' should be in the plural.}}

'''biokinetics''' (bī″ō-ki-net′iks), ''n.'' [< Gr. βίος, life, + ''kinetics''.] That part of biological science which treats of the successive changes through which organisms pass during the different stages of their development.

'''biologian''' (bī-ọ-lō′jia̤n), ''n.'' [< ''biology'' + ''-ian''.] A biologist.
{{smaller block|Those great classes into which systematists and ''biologians'' have divided existing vertebrate forms.}}

